  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nCurrently a very elongated ridge exists across the southern \nCONUS with zonal to WNW mid level flow in the northern CONUS. As\nwe progress through the week ridging over the four corners into\nTexas panhandle will amplify at the same time PNW troughing \nbegins to turn flow more zonal over us as the ridge gets pushed \neast. By Thursday SW flow looks to dominate the western CONUS \nbringing pacific moisture over the Rockies with embedded \nshortwaves. Certainly still differences remaining across \nclusters but the potential for a wet late week/weekend looks to \nbe increasing. The current placement of synoptic features would \nmore heavily favor southern areas for a soaking rain but even \nthen it looks to only be a quarter to half inch (locally 1\" \nperhaps) from Friday through Saturday.\n\n- Severe threat today\n\nWhile parts of southeast North Dakota are outlooked for this \nevening the main risk looks to stay well south of the area into \nSouth Dakota as a potential MCS develops amid a seasonably moist\nand unstable airmass with dewpoints in the low 70s across much \nof the Missouri river corridor in South Dakota. Further north \ntowards Bismarck dewpoints should still manage to climb into the\nmid to upper 60s though this will only allow for 2000-2500 \nMUCAPE compared to the 3000-3500 further south with shear \nrelatively equal across the warm sector. Given cloud layer shear\napproaching or even exceeding 70kts and DCAPE in excess of 1300\nj/kg upscale growth seems to be the likely storm mode with an \ninitial window for large hail and a few tornadoes possible in \nSouth Dakota. Should storms clip our area the thought is upscale\ngrowth would already be underway and winds up to 60mph would be\nthe primary threat. \n\n- Severe threat Tuesday\n\nOn Tuesday parts of southeast North Dakota are once again \noutlooked in a marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for large hail \nand damaging wind gusts. The primary initiating boundary looks \nto be more so in South Dakota as a cold front moves southeast \nwith no defined waves aloft for large scale forcing. This will \nincrease the uncertainty in location of storms due to the subtle\nnature of the forcing but with around 2000 MLCAPE along and \nsouth of I-94 and eff shear of 40- 50kts, rotating storms are \ncertainly possible. Perhaps we simply reach convective Ts in the\nmid 80s for some places in southeast North Dakota but lingering\nsmoke aloft could chop a few degrees off our heating potential \npreventing that as well. Overall marginal seems to be the right \ncall here with an equal risk for hail to the size of ping pongs \n(1.5\") and wind gusts to 60 mph with any storms that do form.",
